    art: { src: "/art/geographer.jpg", pos: "45% 35%" },
    body: `The global industry serving ultra-high-net-worth (UHNW) families is going through a fundamental structural shift: the centre of gravity is moving away from classic investment banks and financial advisers towards specialised international law firms and structures run by advocates — so-called lawyer-led family offices.

## The scale of the phenomenon

As of 2026 there are **more than 8,000 family offices** worldwide. The average net worth of respondents among institutional family offices reaches **$1.6 billion**, and annual operating costs of structures with more than $1 billion under management exceed **$6.6 million**.

At this scale a modern family becomes a highly complex cross-border organisation. It lives in several legal dimensions at once: corporate law, international taxation, trust regulation, anti-money-laundering (AML) compliance and the geopolitical mobility of its members.

## Wealth management and a family office are not the same

Traditional wealth management and a full family office differ in scope, depth of coordination and the nature of the fiduciary bond.

Classic wealth management focuses mainly on one question: building a portfolio of liquid securities, planning cash flows and basic tax optimisation inside the investment account — for example, tax-loss harvesting. Financial institutions also tend to sell their own investment or lending products — the so-called product-driven model.

A true family office is built as a **pure fiduciary**: it acts solely for the family and brings under one operating perimeter:

- investments in public and private markets;
- the family's operating business;
- commercial and residential real estate;
- multi-layered trusts and private foundations;
- philanthropy;
- intra-family governance.

## The trigger is complexity, not size

Market analysis shows that the decisive factor for moving to a family office model is not the absolute size of net assets but the **structural complexity of the wealth**.

A family whose wealth sits entirely in one brokerage account can be served well by a classic wealth manager. A family that owns an operating business, a rental property portfolio, direct investments (private equity, venture capital, private credit) and a network of trusts and partnerships (LLC/LP) needs complete legal and tax synchronisation — even with less liquidity.

> In a decentralised set-up the investment adviser, the tax specialist and the estate lawyer each see only isolated fragments. An aggressive deduction taken by the accountant this year can, a few years later, trigger critical tax liabilities or a breach of trust covenants — if the structure has no single architect.

## The advocate as the family's quarterback

It is this need for end-to-end architectural coordination that has made advocates the quarterback — the general operator — of the family office. Unlike financial managers who use standardised online platforms for template estate planning, an advocate-led family office practice is built from the outset on **designing a protective legal framework**.

Leading practices — McDermott Will & Emery, Withers, Macfarlanes, Mishcon de Reya, Ropes & Gray — manage family wealth on three interconnected tiers at once.

## Three tiers of family wealth

| Tier | What it includes |
|---|---|
| Upper — beneficiaries and ownership | Individuals and family members, private trust companies (PTCs), dynastic trusts, private charitable foundations |
| Middle — the family office and investment platforms | The office's own operating entities, family investment companies (FICs), holdings, variable capital companies (VCCs), co-investment vehicles |
| Lower — underlying assets and transactions | Controlling and minority stakes in operating businesses, direct M&A deals, venture and PE investments, commercial real estate, trophy assets: art, yachts, business aviation, equestrian estates |

The core of this structure is the advocate-led family office in one of four forms: outside counsel, a dedicated consultancy, a trust corporation or in-house General Counsel. The core receives its mandate from the upper tier and reports to it, and directs the lower tier through instructions while receiving its reporting. All of this happens inside the **perimeter of advocate secrecy and fiduciary control** — why that perimeter is so valuable, and how not to lose it, is the subject of a separate article.`,
